`Invoice` mixing business rules with formatting and storage — three reasons to change, one class.

```text
// Violation: one class, three reasons to change
class Invoice:
    function calculateTotal()   // pricing rules
    function printToPdf()       // formatting/output
    function saveToDatabase()   // persistence

// Fix: split by responsibility
class Invoice:
    function calculateTotal()

class InvoicePrinter:
    function printToPdf(invoice)

class InvoiceRepository:
    function save(invoice)
```

Now a change to PDF layout can't accidentally break pricing or storage.

A discount calculator that needs editing every time a new customer type appears.

```text
// Violation: must edit this function for every new type
function getDiscount(customerType):
    if customerType == "REGULAR": return 0.0
    if customerType == "PREMIUM": return 0.1
    // adding "VIP" means editing this function again

// Fix: extend via new types, don't modify existing code
interface DiscountPolicy:
    function getDiscount()

class RegularDiscount implements DiscountPolicy:
    function getDiscount() -> 0.0

class PremiumDiscount implements DiscountPolicy:
    function getDiscount() -> 0.1

// Adding VIP = add a new class, zero edits above
class VipDiscount implements DiscountPolicy:
    function getDiscount() -> 0.2
```

The textbook trap: `Square` extending `Rectangle` breaks callers that resize width and height independently.

```text
// Violation: Square "is-a" Rectangle in real life, not in behavior
class Rectangle:
    function setWidth(w)
    function setHeight(h)

class Square extends Rectangle:
    function setWidth(w):  width = height = w   // breaks independence
    function setHeight(h): width = height = h

// Caller assumes width/height are independent — Square silently violates that
function resize(Rectangle r):
    r.setWidth(5)
    r.setHeight(10)
    // expects area 50; gets 100 for a Square

// Fix: don't force the inheritance — model what each shape can do
interface Shape:
    function area()

class Rectangle implements Shape: ...
class Square implements Shape: ...
```

A fat `Worker` interface forces a `Robot` to implement `eat()`, which makes no sense for it.

```text
// Violation: one fat interface for every kind of worker
interface Worker:
    function work()
    function eat()

class Robot implements Worker:
    function work()
    function eat()  // forced to implement; throws or no-ops

// Fix: split by capability
interface Workable:
    function work()

interface Feedable:
    function eat()

class HumanWorker implements Workable, Feedable: ...
class Robot implements Workable: ...   // no eat() forced on it
```

`OrderService` hard-wired to `MySqlDatabase` — swapping databases means editing business logic.

```text
// Violation: high-level policy tied to a low-level concrete class
class OrderService:
    db = new MySqlDatabase()   // hard-coded detail
    function placeOrder(order): db.save(order)

// Fix: depend on an abstraction; inject the concrete detail
interface Database:
    function save(order)

class MySqlDatabase implements Database: ...
class InMemoryDatabase implements Database: ...   // e.g. for tests

class OrderService:
    constructor(Database db): this.db = db
    function placeOrder(order): db.save(order)

// OrderService never changes when the storage detail changes
```
